In C, you can terminate a program by calling the standard library function `exit()`. This function takes an argument that specifies how the program should terminate:

- `EXIT_SUCCESS` indicates that the program has terminated successfully.
- `EXIT_FAILURE` indicates that the program has encountered an error.

Here's an example of how to use `exit()`:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main ( void ) {
    int * p_x, * p_y;
    size_t n_x, n_y;
    double slope;

    if ( scanf ( "<STR_LIT>" , & n_x , & p_x , & n_y , & p_y ) != 5 ) {
        fprintf ( stderr , "<STR_LIT>" ) ;
        exit ( EXIT_FAILURE ) ;
    }

    slope = ( double ) ( p_x - p_y ) / ( double ) ( n_x - n_y ) ;
    printf ( "<STR_LIT>" , slope ) ;
    exit ( EXIT_SUCCESS ) ;
}
```
In this example, the program reads data from the standard input and computes the slope of a line. If the input data is invalid, the program terminates with an error. Otherwise, the program terminates successfully.

You can also use `atexit()` to register a function that gets called when the program terminates. This function can be used to clean up resources before the program ends.

Here's an example of how to use `atexit()`:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

void cleanup ( void ) {
    /* Perform any necessary cleanup actions. */
}

int main ( void ) {
    int c;
    FILE * input = stdin ;
    FILE * output = stdout ;

    if ( atexit ( cleanup ) != 0 ) {
        fprintf ( stderr , "<STR_LIT>" ) ;
        exit ( EXIT_FAILURE ) ;
    }

    while ( ( c = getc ( input ) ) != EOF ) {
        putc ( c , output ) ;
    }
    if ( ferror ( input ) || ferror ( output ) ) {
        fprintf ( stderr , "<STR_LIT>" ) ;
        exit ( EXIT_FAILURE ) ;
    }
    atexit ( cleanup ) ;
    exit ( EXIT_SUCCESS ) ;
}
```
